WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.220
Can one click move our
money or open our network?

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.850
Phishing plays a person in four ways.

00:00:07.217 --> 00:00:09.727
First, a login page that is not ours.

00:00:10.087 --> 00:00:13.277
An employee types a password
into a fake sign-in.

00:00:13.447 --> 00:00:16.337
Then the real site loads,
so nobody notices.

00:00:16.762 --> 00:00:21.332
Consider passkeys or hardware keys,
because they refuse the wrong site.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.280
Second, the second factor
gets talked out of the door.

00:00:25.555 --> 00:00:29.885
At 22:40, the phone asks to
approve a sign-in from Amsterdam.

00:00:30.475 --> 00:00:32.145
The third prompt gets approved.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.160
Consider a phishing-resistant
second factor, number matching,

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:37.820
and a limit on prompts.

00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:40.987
Third, a request from the CEO.

00:00:41.442 --> 00:00:44.032
On Friday at 16:50,

00:00:44.102 --> 00:00:47.812
finance is told to pay
240,000 euros today.

00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:51.437
The money is gone in hours,
and no filter catches it.

00:00:51.642 --> 00:00:56.112
Consider a call-back on a known number,
and four eyes on every bank change.

00:00:57.117 --> 00:00:58.527
Fourth, beyond email.

00:00:58.887 --> 00:01:02.617
Text messages, phone calls,
QR codes and chat apps.

00:01:02.907 --> 00:01:06.537
They sit outside our mail filters,
often on a private phone.

00:01:06.812 --> 00:01:11.282
Consider controls that work on any
channel, and a report button people use.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:13.560
What good looks like.

00:01:13.845 --> 00:01:16.035
Someone will click, probably this month.

00:01:16.365 --> 00:01:17.235
That is arithmetic,

00:01:17.725 --> 00:01:18.795
not a training failure.

00:01:19.175 --> 00:01:23.325
The question is whether one click can
move our money or open our network.

00:01:23.685 --> 00:01:27.295
If so, the fix is a passkey
and a call-back procedure,

00:01:27.645 --> 00:01:29.315
not another awareness poster.

00:01:31.297 --> 00:01:32.507
A suggested first step.

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:35.862
Ask the CFO and the CIO for four numbers.

00:01:36.222 --> 00:01:38.692
The report rate within
one hour of a simulation,

00:01:39.262 --> 00:01:40.972
and the click rate as a trend.

00:01:41.347 --> 00:01:44.557
The share of staff on a
phishing-resistant second factor.

00:01:44.812 --> 00:01:48.422
The share of payments and bank
changes verified by call-back.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.900
One click should not move money.

00:01:52.225 --> 00:01:53.835
Use passkeys, and a call-back.

